# factory-reach-endpoints.env — the endpoints ./verify-factory-reach.sh probes
# from inside a `factory`-namespace pod (livespec-dev-tooling-sszvvw, livespec
# plan factory-worker-nodes-gitops, epic livespec-sqvj7p, plan assertion 7).
#
# FORMAT: one `NAME=HOST:PORT` per line; `#` comments and blank lines ignored.
# NAME is lowercase `[a-z0-9-]` and is what the check reports when a probe
# fails, so it is the operator-facing identity of the endpoint. The renderer
# refuses a malformed line rather than rendering a Job that probes nonsense.
#
# WHY A COMMITTED FILE RATHER THAN LITERALS IN THE SCRIPT. The work-item
# requires the endpoints to be arguments or committed configuration and never
# memorised. A literal in the script body would make the probe set invisible to
# the operator reading `--dry-run`, and an operator probing a different endpoint
# would have to EDIT the check — which is how a check comes to prove something
# other than what it says. Override one entry with
# `--endpoint NAME=HOST:PORT`, or replace this file with `--endpoints FILE`.
#
# BOTH ENTRIES ARE TAILNET ADDRESSES, AND ./factory-namespace.yaml FORCES THAT.
# Its `factory-egress` NetworkPolicy allows exactly three egress classes:
# cluster DNS, the whole Tailscale CGNAT range `100.64.0.0/10` at any port, and
# TCP 443 off-site with every private range EXCEPTED. So an endpoint named by a
# homelab LAN address (192.168.1.0/24), by a node's own cni0 gateway
# (10.42.0.0/16, which is what a pod's default route resolves to) or by a
# cluster Service address is DENIED to a factory pod by construction, however
# reachable it is from the host. ./DERIVATION.md "The tailnet" derives that rule
# from these same two endpoints.
#
# beads-dolt-tenant — this repository's per-repo beads/Dolt tenant on the shared
# family dolt-server. The PORT is committed in this repository's own
# `.beads/config.yaml` as `dolt.server-port` (mirrored by `.livespec.jsonc`'s
# impl-plugin connection block). The HOST is NOT: that file commits
# `dolt.server-host: 127.0.0.1`, which is correct for the host-side dispatcher
# that reaches the tenant through the `with-livespec-env.sh` credential wrapper
# and names the READER'S OWN POD when handed to a pod. The address below is the
# dispatcher host's tailnet address, measured 2026-10-13 and recorded in
# ./DERIVATION.md "The tailnet".
#
# EXPECT THIS PROBE TO FAIL UNTIL THE TENANT PUBLISHES A TAILNET LISTENER, and
# that result is the measurement this check exists to take rather than a defect
# in it: on 2026-10-13 TCP to `127.0.0.1:3307`, `172.17.0.1:3307` AND
# `100.89.189.118:3307` was REFUSED from inside a live hp Docker sandbox,
# because the server binds loopback on whichever tailnet member hosts it.
# ./DERIVATION.md defers the per-node reach question to this work-item by name.
# A red probe names the endpoint and the port; a green one means a listener was
# added. Neither is a reason to edit this file.
beads-dolt-tenant=100.89.189.118:3307
#
# telemetry-receiver — the OTLP receiver a dispatch projects into its sandbox as
# `LIVESPEC_SANDBOX_OTEL_ENDPOINT`, read from inside a live hp Docker sandbox on
# 2026-10-13 as `http://100.89.189.118:35129`, where `POST /v1/traces` answered
# HTTP 200. That is the receiver the hp Docker sandboxes use today, which is the
# one this check was asked to prove a pod can reach.
#
# ITS PORT IS THE DISPATCHER'S EPHEMERAL BOUND PORT, so the value below is a
# RECORD of one dispatch rather than a durable address — the same fact that
# keeps any port literal out of ./factory-namespace.yaml's tailnet egress rule.
# For a live run, read the value a current dispatch projects and pass
# `--endpoint telemetry-receiver=HOST:PORT`. Left as committed it proves the
# tailnet PATH to the dispatcher host and reports the port as unreachable once
# that dispatcher is gone, which is the honest answer for an address that no
# longer exists.
#
# NOT the CI lane's receiver, and not the host collector's docker-bridge
# listener. ../arc/hook-pod-template.yaml posts a CI runner pod's spans to the
# host collector's keyless `otlp/pods` listener on each node's own cni0 gateway
# at 4319, and `ansible/roles/fabro_server/files/otel.conf` names
# `http://172.17.0.1:4318` for a Docker sandbox beside its dispatcher. Both
# addresses are inside ranges the `factory-egress` policy excepts, so neither is
# an endpoint a factory pod may reach at all.
telemetry-receiver=100.89.189.118:35129
